WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.037 --> 00:00:02.477
- "Val, there are three
shots in your pistol yet!

00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:03.697
"You had better discharge them."

00:00:03.697 --> 00:00:04.530
"What for?"

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.547
"To prevent any accident.

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:06.617
"You might shoot yourself."

00:00:06.617 --> 00:00:08.570
"No danger of that," was the reply.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.870
Evidently, the writers
of "Law &amp; Order 1871"

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.055
were really into very
obvious foreshadowing.

00:00:15.055 --> 00:00:16.950
(tense music)

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.160
Among the many sworn duties of lawyers,

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.060
perhaps the most famous is
that of zealous advocate.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.710
A lawyer is duty-bound to
do everything reasonable,

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.020
and ethical, to help a
client achieve their goals.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.840
The best lawyers burn
the candle at both ends

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.370
with long hours and little sleep

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.480
in service of zealous representation,

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.530
figuratively killing
themselves for their clients.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.310
But do you know the story of perhaps

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.310
the greatest defense attorney of all time,

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.430
who took the oath of zealous advocacy

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.020
just a little too seriously

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.400
by not figuratively dying for his client,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.410
but literally dying for his client?

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.890
Well, today, we're going to discuss

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.530
the curious case of Clement Vallandigham,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.290
whose novel legal defense got his client

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.540
acquitted of murder charges
at the cost of his own life,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:03.130
and along the way, was found
guilty of treason and exiled,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.630
before sneaking back into
this country in disguise.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.710
It's going to be a wild ride,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:08.710
so let's get started.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:11.600
(perky music)

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.360
Brought to you by Trade Coffee.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.000
Clement Vallandigham was
born on July 29th, 1820,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.300
in New Lisbon, Ohio,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.960
now just Lisbon, Ohio.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.700
Growing up, he was best
friends with Edwin M. Stanton,

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.610
who would become the Secretary of War

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.350
under President Abraham Lincoln.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.460
Growing up, Vallandigham was
close friends with Stanton,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.500
until a falling out was caused,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.860
not by stealing each other's toys,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.120
but being on opposing
sides of the Civil War,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.320
as one does.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.450
But when they were still friends,

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.390
Stanton lent Vallandigham
$500 for a law course

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.450
and to begin his own practice.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.450
And after a short stint in
the Ohio State Legislature,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.940
Vallandigham ran for Congress
as a Democrat in 1856.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.070
Despite being narrowly
defeated by 19 votes,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.720
Vallandigham appealed to
the Committee of Elections

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.160
of the House of Representatives,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.490
claiming illegal votes denied him victory.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.390
After nearly two years of deliberation,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.500
the House eventually agreed,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.200
and Vallandigham was seated
for a two-day House term.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.730
Thankfully, we've come
a long way since 1856,

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.160
where politicians just gracefully accept

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.137
the results of a lost election.

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.400
(crickets chirping)

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.330
Vallandigham was elected
to a full term in 1858

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.080
and reelected in 1860,

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.700
before being defeated
in the 1862 election.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.600
When the US Civil War broke
out, Congressman Vallandigham

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.410
became the de facto
leader of the Copperheads,

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.080
a group of antiwar Midwest Union Democrats

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.770
who opposed the US Civil War,

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.380
opposed the abolition of slavery

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.290
and demanded an immediate peace settlement

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.500
with the Confederacy.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.270
And, yeah, yeah, yeah, for anyone trolling

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:42.040
in the comments section, the
Democrats and the Republicans

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.640
of the 1860s were not the same

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.330
as the Democrats and Republicans of today,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.520
so, just don't.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.200
But the Copperheads
actively resisted the draft

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:52.430
and encouraged Union soldiers
to desert their units.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.750
Both in and out of Congress,
Vallandigham became

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.070
one of Abraham Lincoln's
most outspoken critics,

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.780
which managed to get
Vallandigham in a bit of trouble.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.300
- There's a good chance
I may have committed

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.950
some light treason.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.460
- But as the Civil War dragged on,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.080
Abraham Lincoln began
undertaking some extraordinary,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.790
and in retrospect, legally
questionable measures

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.600
to crack down on war opponents.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.810
In 1862, Lincoln issued a proclamation

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.180
suspending Habeas Corpus,

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.680
meaning that if you were detained,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.560
you could no longer seek
a remedy in the courts

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.470
to determine if the
detention was lawful or not.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.130
The proclamation also made
discouraging enlistment,

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.510
resisting drafts, or any
other disloyal practice

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.830
subject to martial law and
trial by a military commission.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:33.340
But enter Major General Ambrose Burnside,

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.540
famous for humiliating military defeats

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.540
and for making sideburns a thing.

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.410
I thought I told you to cut
those sideburns, Burnside.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:42.430
- What sideburns?

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.730
- You heard me, hippie.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:45.970
- But after losing the
Battle of Fredericksburg

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.670
and the Battle of Crater,
Lincoln demoted Burnside,

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.200
and sentenced him to Ohio,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.920
where he quickly made a name for himself.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.820
A few weeks after
Vallandigham returned to Ohio,

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.450
Ambrose Burnside made his move

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.210
against the rebel Copperheads.

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:03.310
In April 1863, Burnside issued
General Order number 38,

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.640
which declared Jedi's
traders to the Republic,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.430
leading to the Great Jedi Purge.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.760
No, no, that's, that's not what happened.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.910
Sorry, wrong Civil War.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.380
But what Burnside's General
Order number 38 did,

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.290
was to make it a crime, in some
cases, punishable by death,

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.440
to express public opposition to the war.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.837
The order stated, "The
habit of declaring sympathy

00:04:21.837 --> 00:04:24.427
"for the enemy will not be
allowed in this department.

00:04:24.427 --> 00:04:26.927
"Persons committing such
offenses will be at once arrested

00:04:26.927 --> 00:04:28.417
"with a view of being tried

00:04:28.417 --> 00:04:31.407
"or sent beyond our lines into
the lines of their friends.

00:04:31.407 --> 00:04:34.487
"It must be understood that
treason, expressed or implied,

00:04:34.487 --> 00:04:36.960
"will not be tolerated
in this department."

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.800
And of course, the beatings will continue

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.320
until morale improves.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.710
But on May 1st, 1863, now
ex-Congressman Vallandigham

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.830
organized a rally in Mount Vernon, Ohio,

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.000
denouncing the order,
the war, and demanding

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.860
the immediate removal of King Lincoln.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.570
And in his speech, Vallandigham,

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:55.000
unaware that union agents were
in the audience taking notes,

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.060
asserted, among other
things, that the Civil War

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.100
was a sinister plot not
to preserve the Union,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.880
but to free slaves and
enslave whites in their place,

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.410
and thank goodness, we're
a long way past the days

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.639
of public officials who spout
baseless conspiracy theories.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:08.900
(crickets chirping)

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:10.700
Vallandigham asserted the Constitution

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.630
protected his right to speak
freely despite the order.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.550
Ambrose Burnside disagreed.

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.469
And in the early hours of May 5th, 1863,

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:21.300
150 Union soldiers surrounded
Vallandigham's home

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.840
and arrested the former
Congressman for treason.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.060
And as a result, Vallandigham
was brought before

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:28.970
a military tribunal, where
he refused to enter a plea.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.530
Ever the lawyer, he argued
that the military tribunal

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.280
lacked jurisdiction to hear his case,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.080
as he was not a member of the military

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.570
nor a resident of a state in rebellion,

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.740
or in a state under martial law.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.780
And Vallandigham contended
that the Constitution

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.750
guaranteed his right to have
his case heard in a civil court

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:46.990
in front of a jury of his peers,

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.910
guaranteed under the
Constitution, clear as day.

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.110
The military tribunal disagreed.

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.557
Vallandigham was convicted of quote,

00:05:53.557 --> 00:05:55.080
"Uttering disloyal sentiments,"

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.520
and attempting to hinder
prosecution of the war,

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.080
and sentenced to two years
in a military prison.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:01.830
Vallandigham actually appealed his case

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.070
all the way to the Supreme Court,

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.450
arguing that as a civilian,

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:06.840
he could not be tried
as a military tribunal.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.020
Which gave rise to the Ex
parte Vallandigham case.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.540
In Ex parte Vallandigham,
the Supreme Court,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.540
sidestepping Vallandigham's
jurisdictional questions,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.390
unanimously ruled that the
Supreme Court had no power

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.430
to even hear appeals
from military tribunals.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.030
Although Vallandigham lost
this case, after the war,

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.200
the Supreme Court did
vindicate his position.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.370
In a similar case, Ex parte Milligan,

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.410
the Supreme Court ruled
that trying civilians

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.820
in military tribunals in unconstitutional

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.640
when civilian courts are available.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.090
So, what happened next?

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.780
Well, Abraham Lincoln commuted
Vallandigham's sentence

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.400
and banished him to the
Confederacy, of all places.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.460
Vallandigham though,
then sneaked into Canada,

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.160
ran for Governor of Ohio, from
a hotel in Ontario, and lost,

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:50.410
and then became the leader
of the paramilitary group

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.250
the Order of the Sons of Liberty,

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.910
with a plan of creating
the Northwest Confederacy

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.150
by overthrowing the state
governments of Ohio,

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.840
Kentucky, Indiana and Illinois.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.640
He was anything, if not ambitious.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:06.160
But in June 1864, Vallandigham,
under heavy disguise,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.000
sneaked back into the US.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.050
The intended revolt never materialized,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.340
though several other members

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.730
of the Order of the Sons of Liberty

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.200
were convicted of
conspiracy to commit treason

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.730
by a military tribunal.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:19.110
Where, of course,
Vallandigham gave testimony,

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:21.120
only to have their own
tribunal convictions

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.330
thrown out by the Supreme Court,

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:24.490
by you guessed, it Ex parte Milligan.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.360
But after failed bids
in the Senate and House,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.330
Vallandigham resumed his law practice.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.570
And this is where things
get really interesting,

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.190
because, alas, twas the
overzealous lawyering

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:36.880
that killed the Congressman.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:40.500
In 1871, Clement Vallandigham
was halfway through trying

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.580
what he thought would be the
greatest case of his life,

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.350
but it actually turned out to be

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.720
the last case of his entire life.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.810
Enter his client, one Thomas McGehan,

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:51.120
who had been charged with the murder

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.290
of a well-known local tough guy
and scoundrel, Thomas Myers.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:57.620
And one fateful Christmas
Eve in Hamilton, Ohio,

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:00.300
Meyers had been playing cards
in a private room in a bar,

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.170
where five men burst into the room

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:03.960
and a bar room brawl broke out.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.720
As Meyers rose from his seat,

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.480
rummaging in his pocket
to draw his pistol,

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:08.860
a muffled shot was hurt.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.980
Meyers drew his gun, fired a couple shots

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:12.820
before he collapsed dead.

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:14.270
Those who witnessed the chaos

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:17.220
kept bringing up one murder
suspect, Thomas McGehan,

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.660
with whom Myers had a long-running feud.

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.410
But the defense's theory
was that Myers had in fact

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.710
accidentally shot himself
in all of the ruckus.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.920
And ever the hands-on lawyer,
Vallandigham was dead set

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.640
on proving this theory
correct to his co-counsel.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.650
So, like any good defense attorney,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:37.440
Vallandigham began
inspecting the murder weapon.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.310
And after firing two rounds

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.730
of his five-shot Smith and Wesson revolver

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.130
into a square of cloth,

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.250
to test whether it would
leave a mark of powder,

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.200
Vallandigham headed back to
the hotel with his co-counsel.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.360
Newspaper accounts at the time

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.020
recounted the following conversation

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:54.300
that would portend
Vallandigham's impending demise.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.777
Co-counsel Milliken, ever
cautious and thoughtful said,

00:08:56.777 --> 00:08:59.227
"Val, there are three
shots in your pistol yet!

00:08:59.227 --> 00:09:00.467
"You had better discharge them."

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:02.517
"What for," responded Mr. Vallandigham.

00:09:02.517 --> 00:09:05.247
"To prevent any accident,"
replied the cautious attorney.

00:09:05.247 --> 00:09:06.307
"You might shoot yourself."

00:09:06.307 --> 00:09:08.767
"No danger of that,"
replied Mr.Vallandigham.

00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:10.757
"I carried and practiced with pistols

00:09:10.757 --> 00:09:13.697
"too long to be afraid to have
a loaded one in my pocket."

00:09:13.697 --> 00:09:15.867
"You'd better be careful,
though," said Mr. Milliken.

00:09:15.867 --> 00:09:18.110
"Never fear me," was the reply.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:21.410
Evidently the writers
of "Law &amp; Order 1871"

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.816
were really into very
obvious foreshadowing.

00:09:24.816 --> 00:09:26.560
(tense music)

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.610
And when Vallandigham
returned to the hotel,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.990
he received a package,
an unloaded revolver

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.950
that had been used as a trial exhibit.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:34.670
Back in the hotel room,
Vallandigham placed

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:37.200
both weapons on the table side by side.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:40.200
And as other lawyers
joined him in the room,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:42.380
Vallandigham proceeded to test his theory

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.660
that Tom Myers shot himself.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.210
Vallandigham, accidentally
grabbing the loaded gun,

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:48.650
because, obviously,

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.360
he placed the revolver in his pocket

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:52.330
and slowly drew it out.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:54.190
He pointed the gun at his abdomen,

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:57.390
in the exact position he
believed Meyers shot himself.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.130
Thinking the gun was unloaded,

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:00.367
he pulled the trigger.

00:10:00.367 --> 00:10:02.470
"My God, I've shot myself,"

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.380
Vallandigham exclaimed, as
he reeled towards the wall.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:08.410
At least four jurors who
were staying at the hotel

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:10.550
entered the room to see
what had transpired.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:12.190
Word spread throughout the town.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:15.070
Maybe there was something
to Vallandigham's theory.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.900
Well, Vallandigham died the next morning.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:21.617
His final words were, "I
may, however, be mistaken,

00:10:21.617 --> 00:10:22.877
"but I am a firm believer

00:10:22.877 --> 00:10:26.770
"in the good old Presbyterian
doctrine of predestination."

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:28.860
So, what happened afterwards?

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.970
Well, after two trials, first a hung jury,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.600
and then a second degree murder conviction

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.490
that was set aside for jury misconduct

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:35.920
and a lack of evidence,

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:38.010
McGehan was acquitted in his third trial.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.840
So, great job, Clement.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:41.480
After his acquittal, McGehan published

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.477
a self-serving
autobiographical work entitled

00:10:43.477 --> 00:10:46.187
"Thomas McGehan, A History
of the Life and Trials

00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:47.570
"of Thomas McGehan."

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.120
McGehan actually used
a print of Vallandigham

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:50.760
shooting himself as the cover.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:52.570
And after being run out of town,

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:55.020
McGehan soon returned to Hamilton, Ohio,

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.090
only to realize he still had enemies.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:59.140
And the universe has
a dark sense of humor.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:02.090
So, after being acquitted for
murder in a bar room brawl

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:04.440
by potentially the greatest
defense attorney of all time,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.110
McGehan would soon meet his end,

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.610
being shot to death in a bar.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.477
As reported by the
whimsically-named "Tiffin Tribune,"

00:11:11.477 --> 00:11:13.387
"The Hamiltonians
threatened to take his life

00:11:13.387 --> 00:11:15.567
"if he attempted to make
his home in that city,

00:11:15.567 --> 00:11:17.867
"and they evidently don't
like to be caught in a lie

00:11:17.867 --> 00:11:19.560
"about a small matter of that kind."

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:21.200
As for Ambrose Burnside,

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:23.060
well, mister sideburns was elected

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.320
to be the first president of the NRA.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:27.880
Yes, that NRA.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:28.870
And that, my friends,

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:31.230
is the curious case of
Clement Vallandigham,

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:34.460
potentially the greatest
defense attorney of all time.

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:36.250
And if there's any takeaways from this,

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.840
it's probably that when
your co-counsel warns you

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.330
not to shoot yourself,

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:42.110
you should probably take that advice.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.800
But this is not the end of the story.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.600
You see, the universe really does have

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:48.940
a dark sense of humor.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.790
And the universe was not finished

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:51.990
with Clement Vallandigham.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:54.117
As reported by the "Fife
Herald" at the time,

00:11:54.117 --> 00:11:55.987
"Hardly was Vallandigham in his grave

00:11:55.987 --> 00:11:58.597
"before another man killed
himself while trying to,

00:11:58.597 --> 00:12:00.187
"precisely in the same way,

00:12:00.187 --> 00:12:03.700
"to demonstrate how Mr.
Vallandigham had met his death."

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.300
I know you think that
lawyers are super smart,

00:12:07.300 --> 00:12:09.860
but some aren't.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:12.100
And while this is a tragic example

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.090
of client advocacy gone wrong,

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:17.040
remember, the 1800s were
a much different time,

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.090
and thankfully, today, we
are much more responsible

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:21.900
in how we handle our firearms.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.727
You might ask yourself,
"How was it possible

00:12:23.727 --> 00:12:25.970
